h=rows['lem-finite-domination-of-surface-modifications-via-relative-hilbert-scheme'];h['statement']=h['statement'].replace('Let $A$ be a regular Noetherian local domain','Let $A$ be a normal Noetherian local domain').replace('There are a regular surface $X$ obtained by finitely many point blowups','There are a normal surface $X$ obtained by finitely many normalized point blowups');h['statement']+=' If $A$ is regular, $X$ is regular and its normalized blowups are ordinary point blowups.';h['strategy']=h['strategy'].replace('since $A$ is regular its normalized point blowups are ordinary regular point blowups. They give a regular projective $X$','these give a normal projective $X$').replace('a regular projective $X$ mapping','a normal projective $X$ mapping');h['strategy']+=' If A is regular, its normalized point blowups are ordinary regular point blowups, giving the stated regular specialization. No step requires regular A before that final specialization.'
sep=add('lem-finite-separable-normal-surface-extension-preserves-bounded-h1','Separable finite surface extensions preserve bounded modification cohomology',['def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1','lem-finite-domination-of-surface-modifications-via-relative-hilbert-scheme','lem-normal-surface-modification-leray-short-exact-sequence','lem-normal-surface-modification-uniform-principal-torsion-bound','lem-trace-pairing-for-a-finite-separable-extension','thm-long-exact-sequence-sheaf-cohomology'],r'''Let $A\subset B$ be a finite injective local extension of permitted normal local surface domains with separable fraction-field extension. If modification H1 over $A$ is uniformly bounded, so is modification H1 over $B$.''',r'''Let L/K have degree n, and choose b1,...,bn∈B forming a K-basis of L. The trace Gram determinant d=det(Tr(bi bj)) lies in A and is nonzero by the published separable trace pairing. For a normal projective modification Y over B, the normal-base Hilbert finite-domination helper gives a dominating normal projective Y′ finite over a normal projective modification X over A. Leray injection embeds the original H1 into H1(Y′). Normality of each target affine algebra makes field trace of every integral element regular there, so Φ:π*O_Y′→O_X^n, s↦(Tr(bi s)) is a sheaf injection. The reverse map (si)↦Σ bi si composed with Φ is its Gram matrix. Its adjugate shows d annihilates coker Φ. The long exact sequence bounds H1(Y′) modulo its d-torsion by H1(X)^n; every element in the kernel is a boundary from H0(coker Φ) and is killed by d. The uniform principal-torsion lemma over B bounds that torsion independently of Y′. The A-length of H1(X)^n is at most n times the assumed A-bound; for finite local B/A, the A-length of a finite-length B-module is [κ(B):κ(A)] times its B-length, as seen on a B-composition series. This gives a uniform B-bound on the quotient and hence on H1(Y′) and H1(Y). General normal proper modifications are handled by projective domination and Leray injection.''')
reflex=add('lem-regular-surface-reflexive-modules-and-codimension-one-lattices','Reflexive surface modules and codimension-one lattice extension',['thm-auslander-buchsbaum-formula','thm-regular-local-rings-are-domains-and-cohen-macaulay','lem-r-one-s-two-intersection-of-height-one-localisations','cor-localisations-of-regular-local-rings-are-regular'],r'''On a regular Noetherian surface a coherent reflexive module is locally free. For a finite module $M$ over a normal Noetherian domain, a generic vector belonging to $M^{**}$ at every height-one localization belongs to $M^{**}$. For a coherent generic-rank-$r$ module on a regular surface, $(\wedge^rM)^{**}$ is the determinant line of $M^{**}$.''',r'''From a finite presentation of M, dualization embeds M* as the kernel of a map between finite free modules. Over a regular local ring of dimension two the image of that map is torsion-free, so has depth at least one; the long exact Ext/depth lemma gives depth(M*)≥2. Apply the same argument again to M**, and Auslander–Buchsbaum makes this finite depth-two module free. In dimensions zero or one the regular local rings are fields or DVRs and finite torsion-free modules are free. A reflexive M equals M**, giving local freeness. For the second assertion write M**=Hom(M*,A). A generic vector v defines a generic functional on M*. If it belongs to all height-one M**, then for every φ∈M*, φ(v) belongs to every height-one A localization and therefore to A by normal intersection. Hence v defines a genuine element of Hom(M*,A)=M**. The same reasoning shows generic maps into a reflexive target extend when regular in codimension one. Modulo torsion, M→M** is an isomorphism at each DVR localization; its quotient is supported at closed points of a surface. Taking top wedges thus agrees in codimension one with det(M**), and the just-proved reflexive extension criterion identifies their double duals with that invertible determinant. The depth lemma here follows directly by applying Ext from the residue field to 0→kernel→free→image→0: Ext0(image)=0, Ext0,1(free)=0, so Ext0,1(kernel)=0.''')
rows[reflex]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[reflex]['deps']+['thm-long-exact-ext-sequence-in-the-second-variable']))
canonical=add('lem-regular-surface-point-blowup-canonical-transform','Canonical modules transform by the exceptional divisor at a regular point blowup',['lem-relative-projective-space-derived-duality-regular-local-base','lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','lem-affine-point-blowup-pushforward-vanishing','lem-regular-base-dualizing-traces-compose-on-rational-modifications','lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base'],r'''For the point blowup $b:X'\to X$ of a regular Noetherian surface in the fixed regular-base dualizing setting, with exceptional divisor $E$, there is a canonical generic-compatible identification $\omega_{X'}=b^*\omega_X\otimes O_{X'}(E)$.''',r'''The assertion is local at the centre. Let T=O_X,x be its two-dimensional regular local ring, choose its parameter pair u,v, and trivialize ω_X locally. The published incidence presentation realizes the blowup as the Cartier hypersurface uV−vU=0 in P1_T. Relative Laurent duality on P1_T gives its canonical complex O(-2)[1] relative to the base dualizing module T[2]. Cartier coinduction from the hypersurface's O(-1)→O resolution multiplies the canonical line by O(1) and subtracts one shift; the resulting surface module is O(-1) times the pulled-back base line. The tautological O(1) on the blowup is O(-E), so O(-1)=O(E). Coinduction and Laurent evaluation preserve the generic identification. Outside the centre b is an isomorphism. These identifications glue: on overlaps they induce the same identification of duality pairings, and the representing-property uniqueness of the regular-base dualizing helper forces equality. This is a local Koszul/Laurent computation; it does not assert an unproved formula for a blowup at a singular point.''')
comparison=add('lem-positive-characteristic-top-differentials-map-to-blown-up-canonical-module','Top differential lattices map into point-blowup canonical modules',[reflex,canonical,'thm-conormal-exact-sequence-algebra','def-kahler-differentials-algebra','def-sheaf-relative-differentials'],r'''Let $A$ be a regular local surface of characteristic $p$, and let $A_0\subset A$ have coherent differential module $\Omega_{A/A_0}$ free of finite rank $r$. Choose $\omega_A=\wedge^r\Omega_{A/A_0}$. Every finite sequence of regular point blowups $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ has a generic-compatible map $(\wedge^r\Omega_{X/A_0})^{**}\to\omega_X$.''',r'''Induct through one point blowup b:X′→X. Let F=Ω_X/A0 modulo torsion and let V=F**; the preceding regular-surface helper makes V a vector bundle of rank r, and D_X=(∧^rΩ_X/A0)**=det V. On a standard point-blowup chart T[t]/(ut−v), the relative differential presentation is generated by dt with relation u dt=0 (and v dt=t u dt). Hence Ω_X′/X is the exceptional curve differential line, locally O_E dt; its stalk at the generic point of E has length one over the exceptional DVR. At that DVR, Ω_X′/A0 modulo torsion is a free lattice F′ of rank r. The image J of b*F in it has full rank and quotient of length at most one, since it is a quotient of Ω_X′/X. Under the generic identification, J is also the image lattice of b*F in b*V, and is contained in that free lattice; its index there has length d≥0. Determinants over the DVR give det F′=det(b*V) multiplied by a scalar of valuation d−length(F′/J)≥−1. Thus the top differential line on X′ is contained in b*D_X(E) along E, and equals b*D_X away from E, where b is an isomorphism. The codimension-one reflexive-extension criterion makes this a global generic-compatible inclusion D_X′→b*D_X(E). Compose it with the inductive map b*D_X(E)→b*ω_X(E) and the regular-point canonical transform identification to obtain D_X′→ω_X′. The initial map is the chosen equality on Spec A. This explicit lattice bound accommodates torsion and failure of local freeness in Ω_X; it does not assume the source's terse either/or determinant formula without proving the needed bound.''')
traceform=add('lem-degree-p-inseparable-differential-trace-extends-on-normal-surfaces','Degree-p differential trace extends across normal surface valuations',[reflex,'thm-height-one-localisation-of-normal-noetherian-domain-is-dvr','thm-conormal-exact-sequence-algebra','def-kahler-differentials-algebra','thm-nakayama-lemma','lem-surface-modification-isomorphism-in-codimension-one'],r'''Let $Y\xrightarrow\pi X$ be a finite dominant morphism of normal integral Noetherian schemes of characteristic $p$ whose function fields have purely inseparable degree $p$. If $\Omega_{X/S}$ is coherent, then for $q\ge1$ the generic differential trace extends canonically to $\pi_*\wedge^q\Omega_{Y/S}\to(\wedge^q\Omega_{X/S})^{**}$. For a monogenic algebra $B=A[z]/(z^p-f)$ it kills forms pulled back from $A$ and sends $\eta\wedge z^i dz$ to zero for $i<p-1$ and to $\eta\wedge df$ for $i=p-1$.''',r'''For a monogenic algebra its differential presentation is (Ω_A/S⊗B⊕B dz)/(B df). Thus modulo forms entirely from A, the exterior quotient is (∧^(q−1)(Ω_A/S/A df))⊗Ω_B/A. Define the map by wedging a lift η with df and taking the coefficient of z^(p−1)dz; changing η by a df term does not change that wedge. This proves well-definedness and the stated formula without a cotangent-complex import. Independence of z follows by the universal polynomial calculation: for z=Σ_(i<p)λ_i w^i and w^p=g, one has f=Σλ_i^p g^i. For j<p−1, z^j ∂z/∂w is (j+1)^−1∂(z^(j+1))/∂w, whose coefficients of w^(lp−1) vanish. For j=p−1, lift the universal coefficients to Z and compute p^−1∂(z^p)/∂w; its w^(p−1) coefficient after w^p=g is Σ_(i=1)^(p−1)iλ_i^p g^(i−1), whose product with dg is df. Pure base forms are killed. The wedge-quotient construction is intrinsic, so this coordinate calculation proves the same independence for higher forms. To extend over X, test height-one DVRs A=O_X,x. The finite integral closure B in the degree-p field extension is a local DVR: purely inseparable extensions have unique primes, and normality gives the DVR property. B is torsion-free finite over A, hence free of rank p. If e is the ramification index and f_res the residue degree, length_A(B/π_A B)=p=e f_res, as the successive powers of B's uniformizer have residue field degree f_res. Thus either e=p, f_res=1 or e=1, f_res=p. In the first case a B-uniformizer z has z^p∈A a uniformizer; its powers generate B/π_A B and Nakayama gives B=A[z]. In the second case lift a residue-field primitive element z; z^p belongs to A by normality, the p residue powers form a basis, and Nakayama again gives B=A[z]. The monogenic formula therefore takes regular forms to Ω_A/S at every height-one localization. Coherent relative differentials on Y follow from the exact differential sequence and finiteness. The double-dual codimension-one criterion now extends and glues the generic map. It is canonical since its target injects into the generic module. This supplies the DVR basis and coordinate-invariance details that are necessary for the source trace extension.''')
insep=add('lem-complete-regular-surface-degree-p-extension-has-bounded-h1','Degree-p inseparable extensions of complete regular surfaces have bounded H1',['lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','lem-finite-domination-of-surface-modifications-via-relative-hilbert-scheme',comparison,traceform,'lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','lem-normal-surface-trace-cokernel-dualizes-h1-and-bounds-it','lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-regular-base-dualizing-traces-compose-on-rational-modifications'],r'''Let $A=k[[u,v]]$ have characteristic $p>0$, let $L/K$ be a purely inseparable degree-$p$ extension of its fraction field, and let $B$ be the finite normalization of $A$ in $L$. Then normal modification H1 over $B$ is uniformly bounded.''',r'''Write L=K[z]/(z^p-f), clearing denominators by pth powers so f∈A, and let z∈B. The p-basis subfield helper chooses a finite-index k′⊂k such that df is nonzero relative to A0=k′[[u^p,v^p]]. The explicit p-monomial basis identifies Ω_A/A0 as finite free of rank r=[p-basis rank of k/k′]+2: A is the quotient of A0 by adjoining finitely many exponent-p coefficient generators and u,v, whose defining pth-power equations have zero differentials. Choose η of degree r−1 with θ=η∧df nonzero in ω_A=∧^rΩ_A/A0. The normal-surface degree-p trace and finite coinduction give c:∧^rΩ_B/A0→ω_B=Hom_A(B,ω_A), α↦(b↦Tr(bα)). For i=0,...,p−1 take α_i=η∧z^(p−1−i) dz. Its functional on z^j is δ_ij θ: reduce powers using z^p=f and apply the coefficient-p−1 formula. Hence c has full generic image in the rank-one B-module ω_B. Its coherent cokernel is therefore killed by some fixed nonzero d∈B (clear denominators for finitely many generators). For every normal projective modification Y over B, the regular specialization of Hilbert finite domination gives a dominating normal projective Y′ finite over a regular point-blowup modification X over A. Apply the extended differential trace to Y′→X, then the differential-lattice canonical comparison on X. Finite coinduction and the pairing-preserving dualizing identification give a map ∧^rΩ_Y′/A0→ω_Y′ that generically equals c. Forms from B pull back to global forms on Y′, so the image of Γ(Y′,ω_Y′)→ω_B contains c(∧^rΩ_B/A0): both maps agree generically and ω_B is torsion-free. The trace here is precisely the finite coinduction counit followed by the regular-base trace, whose compatibility follows from the representing-property construction. Thus its cokernel is killed by the fixed d. The trace-cokernel/finite-length duality helper bounds H1(Y′), and Leray injection bounds H1(Y). This proves uniform boundedness without substituting the zero ordinary field trace for the differential trace.''')
rows[insep]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[insep]['deps']+['lem-normal-surface-modification-leray-short-exact-sequence']))
